<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diamond Sutra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vajracchedikā Prajñāpāramitā Sūtra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diamond Sūtra is a Mahāyāna Buddhist scripture of the Prajñāpāramitā, or perfection of wisdom, genre, one of the most influential sutras in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and especially prominent in the Chan or Zen tradition. The date of its composition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> is uncertain, with arguments made for the 2nd and the 5th centuries. Kumārajīva's Chinese translation, thought to date from 401, became the most widely used version. A printed copy of that translation, found among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in 1900 and sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a> in 1907, dates to 868 CE and is now in the British Library; it is the oldest dated printed book known, made, its colophon says, for universal free distribution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra.jpg" alt="Diamond Sutra" width="330" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The colophon, at the inner end, reads: Reverently [caused to be] made for universal free distribution by Wang Jie on behalf of his two paren · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jingangjing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A block-printed copy of the <b>Diamond Sutra</b> was made on this date, according to its colophon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-11">11 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
